we saw this article earlier where  they're making money ball or sports  analytics into a college major at  Syracuse and professor Michael viele the  Syracuse director of the sports  management department kind enough to  join us to talk about this professor  thanks for joining us explain the new  major at Syracuse this ball well good  morning Dan thanks for having me well we  tried to be visionary and  forward-thinking and about three years  ago our department set out on a mission  to figure out where did we see the  sports industry headed in the next  decade or the next quarter century and  there were two common themes that came  about one was technology and the other  one was analytics so we decided to  become the first program in the u.s. to  design a degree a four-year degree based  around sport analytics what will the  curriculum be like the type of classes  well it we've tried to actually look at  where do we see sports setting both  internationally and domestically so  we've incorporated a foreign language  component but the students are going to  take a lot of you cannot economics a lot  of math computer programming to kind of  make an analogy in sports your baseball  players they want to recruit a five-tool  baseball player we want to develop a  five-tool student so they're going to  have emphasis in math economics and  business computer programming for  language and research applications  is there anything with broadcasting  because I find most people who get into  this business as you know first hand mic  that they they still harbor hopes of  being on the air but I love this that it  allows you to have a fallback where you  get a managing degree a marketing degree  that that's going to help you a whole  lot more than wanting to be a  broadcaster well and that that's exactly  the way we've developed the degree dan  any student who walks out of a college  with those five aptitudes the ability to  understand computer programming  master a second language understand  economics computer programming be high  in math and economics  aptitude they're gonna get a job whether  it's in sports and analytics we're  working in big data and analytics or in  the business world and I really think  while all of the emphasis is on  Moneyball and player development and  analyzing players I think the business  side of sports will grow from the  analytic side as much as anything what's  the job market for sports analytics  right now people with analytics  backgrounds have a 27 percent higher job  growth than any other area any other  sector of sports so what you think about  the business side everything deals used  to be done in the golf course people  would sign a seven-figure sponsorship  deal over a round of golf it's no longer  the case everything's got to be  data-driven if a team's going to  increase its profit margin so analytics  will look at food and beverage  consumption patterns of fans it will  look at ticket buying habits of fans it  will look at sponsorship activation  profitability and return on investment  for companies that are associating their  products with sports teams so I think  the business end of this is going to be  every bit as powerful as actually  evaluating players and Syracuse has one  of the great broadcasting schools  Newhouse where you have kids who go  there they want to be broadcasters but  even with that reputation there I tell  kids all the time you know don't go into  the business for money or fame if you go  in because you love sports and you don't  mind working in a smaller market then  maybe the job is for you but what do you  tell these students who come to Syracuse  about the possibility of doing this  professionally as a broadcaster I think  to be a successful student in this  industry will eat you up and spit you  out as you know I mean it it's an  intense uber competitive profession you  have to work during everybody else's  leisure time so you're working  nights weekends and holidays and that's  not for everybody so first of all if  you're going to get into sports you have  to understand it I tell students to  follow your passion if your passion  happens to be take a guy like Matthew  Barrett who graduated from Syracuse and  Newhouse he has a broadcasting degree  but he's made a career out of fantasy  sports so if you pick a niche area and  you can focus on your passion whether  it's entertainment whether it's computer  programming and in the case of this  degree kids who love numbers who love to  sit in front of a computer and crunch  data this is going to create a career  for them and this isn't for everybody  this is going to be a very high aptitude  type of student an Ivy League caliber  student who's got great computer  programming skills but basically wants  to sit in front of a computer run  regression analysis all day and come up  with data that's then going to be turned  over to executives and team owners and  vice presidents and so forth about how  they can run a more efficient  organization Michael do you ever see a  point where we in college teach things  that help you in everyday life whether  it's real estate finances stock market  with these kids who go to college to  play basketball and they know they're  only going to be there for a  one-and-done they're majoring in being a  professional athlete do we get do we  ever have a curriculum that is sort of  geared towards the athlete who says I'm  an athlete and we can help you when you  get into that world a little bit better  than you know taking regular curriculum  it's interesting that you bring that up  we actually teach a course that most of  our student-athletes at Syracuse take  it's a course in personal and  professional development and it gets  into everything about how to manage your  own personal finances how to do basic  everyday kinds of things that we take  for granted but sometimes  student-athletes who are coming from  disadvantaged backgrounds or don't have  a good family situation they've never  been taught these basic skills about  even setting up a base  how to balance a checkbook how does how  to do your finances online those kinds  of things so we have a course that our  student-athletes enroll in that  basically is giving them the foundation  to function as a member of society I  think we need to be teaching more  classes like that yeah I do too but it's  not just you come from a you know a  tough background my kids could use this  when it comes to real estate or finance  or stocks even I mean we all can so I  think that there are things that you  could put into play in the curriculum I  just think the curriculum seems old and  tired in a lot of these universities  well that's why we've tried to be  visionary and become the first program  in the u.s. to actually offer a degree  in analytics because we think it's a  sector that's going to drive the sport  industry both from evaluating talent to  evaluating consumer behavior I mean a  perfect example was Game seven of the  NBA championship when someone went on  StubHub and paid ninety nine thousand  dollars for two courtside seats the days  of people calling the box office of a  stadium are done people are always going  to go to the secondary market and  depending what your price point is  you'll be able to find tickets for  whatever price you're willing to pay and  that's all based on analytics so whether  it's improving the efficiency of food  and beverage whether it's looking at  media returns and sponsorship activation  and so on we think we're really on the  cutting edge of some interesting aspects  of how we can prepare our students for  the next quarter century to work in this  industry Michael thanks for joining us  and good luck with the program Dan we  got to get you back to Syracuse more  often I know you're back for Coach Bay  Himes gala here in April and keep up the  great work thank you thank you Mike  that's professor Michael viele the  Syracuse director of sports management  Department the Dan Patrick show weekday  mornings on audience  you
 

